### 422 — single-leg (degenerate) transaction → domain rule violation
POST {{ledger}}/transactions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    { "accountId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111", "direction": "Debit", "amountMinorUnits": 1000 }
  ],
  "postedOnUtc": "2026-08-29T12:00:00Z"
}

# HTTP/2 422
# content-type: application/problem+json
# {"type":"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4918#section-11.2","title":"The request was well-formed but violates a domain rule.","status":422,"detail":"A transaction must have at least two entries.","code":"Ledger.DegenerateTransaction","traceId":"..."}

### 404 — reversal of an unknown transaction id
# NOTE: swapped from `GET /v1/ledger/accounts/{id}/balance` — that endpoint (GetAccountBalance)
# returns a zeroed 200 Ok for any id, like Parties' balance endpoint, so it never yields a 404.
# The reversal endpoint surfaces Ledger.OriginalTransactionNotFound → 404 through the envelope.
POST {{ledger}}/transactions/99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999/reversal

# HTTP/2 404
# content-type: application/problem+json
# {"type":"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5","title":"The requested resource was not found.","status":404,"detail":"The transaction to reverse was not found.","code":"Ledger.OriginalTransactionNotFound","traceId":"..."}

### 409 — settlement that exceeds the party's outstanding balance
# NOTE: this is the reachable 409-bucket case. The statement-double-pay path
# (Financing.StatementAlreadyPaid → 409) needs: POST /v1/instruments type "credit"
# (cutoffDate set) → POST /v1/financing/payment-plans with a purchaseDate in a
# long-closed cycle → wait for the AccrueInstallments scheduler (interval 1 min, also
# fires on startup) to post the accrual and roll a financing_monthly_statements row →
# POST /v1/financing/statements/{id}/pay once (200) → POST the same again (→ 409).
# Too many timed dependencies for a deterministic manual run; the settlement path
# below exercises the identical envelope + 409 mapping in one call.
#
# Setup: create a fresh party (balance starts at 0), then settle a positive amount.
# @name envParty
POST {{parties}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Envelope Test Party" }

# HTTP/2 201
# content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
# {"id":"01a05dd3-575f-7132-beed-898a4241126e"}

###
POST {{parties}}/{{envParty.response.body.$.id}}/settlements
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amountMinorUnits": 5000,
  "bankAccountId": "22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222",
  "settledOnUtc": "2026-08-31T10:00:00Z"
}

# HTTP/2 409
# content-type: application/problem+json
# {"type":"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.10","title":"The request conflicts with the current state of the resource.","status":409,"detail":"A settlement cannot exceed the party's outstanding balance.","code":"Parties.SettlementExceedsBalance","traceId":"..."}

